Electrical discharge machining of nickel-based superalloys is sensitive to the tool electrode material because electrode properties strongly influence process efficiency, accuracy, and surface quality. Nowicki et al. report that graphite electrodes are increasingly adopted due to low cost, ease of machining, and high sublimation temperature, and that graphite grain size affects machining outcomes: coarser-grained graphite increased material removal rate but raised relative tool wear, while finer-grained graphite produced lower surface roughness. {{cite:S1#c1}} 

A more recent study by the same group refines this finding for Hastelloy C-22 and negative polarity EDM: using POCO AF-5 ($1\ \mu$m) and POCO EDM-180 ($10\ \mu$m) graphite electrodes, they found that fine-grained graphite improves process efficiency and reduces tool wear, and that detached graphite grains can destabilize discharges and thus influence removal mechanisms. {{cite:S3#c1}} These experiments also produced predictive mathematical models relating electrode grain size and machining settings to MRR, relative TWR, and Ra. {{cite:S3#c1}} 

Across studies of high-performance alloys, electrical parameters dominate performance outcomes: peak current and pulse duration (or pulse-on time) repeatedly emerge as primary drivers of MRR and surface roughness. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S2#c1}} In experiments on titanium and other alloys, amperage (peak current) accounted for the largest share of variance in MRR and TWR according to ANOVA analyses reported. {{cite:S8#c1}} 

Electrode geometry and wear behaviour are also critical in practice: in EDM of Hastelloy B2, current and pulse duration strongly influence electrode taper, radius, wear length and cutting time, with sharp-edged square electrodes becoming hemispherical at the ends as a result of unstable sparks and heat diffusion. {{cite:S4#c1}} This illustrates how thermal and geometric transformations of the tool during EDM can undermine dimensional fidelity and increase effective electrode consumption. {{cite:S4#c1}} 

To mitigate high TWR and low MRR inherent to EDM, several adjunct techniques are reported. Combining EDM with cryogenic treatments, powder-mixed dielectric, ultrasonic assistance, and similar methods has been investigated to improve MRR, reduce TWR, and refine surface integrity. {{cite:S6#c1}} Nanopowder-mixed EDM and cryogenically treated electrodes have been explored for other materials, where reported reductions in electrode wear and overcut were attributed to altered discharge behaviour and dielectric properties. {{cite:S7#c1}} Separately, electrical discharge coating (EDC) is presented as a surface-modification variant that can deposit protective or functional layers via discharges, which may be relevant to managing surface integrity in EDM processes. {{cite:S5#c1}} 

Despite these findings, direct experimental comparisons of copper, graphite, and composite electrodes for Hastelloy alloys—quantifying TWR and MRR under controlled parameter sets—are not present in the cited literature. [[NEEDS SOURCE: Comparative data for copper electrodes on Hastelloy]] [[NEEDS SOURCE: Studies of composite electrode performance on Hastelloy]]

rows.bRaw:
Nowicki et al. report that graphite electrodes with larger grain size produced higher process efficiency but also greater relative tool wear when machining Hastelloy C-22, and that the finest surface finish was achieved with the smaller-grain graphite AF-5. {{cite:S1#c1}}  

The same authors later found that fine-grained graphite increased material removal effectiveness and reduced tool wear in negative-polarity EDM of Hastelloy C-22, and they link detached graphite grains to reduced discharge stability and process efficiency. {{cite:S3#c1}} These experiments used POCO AF-5 ($1\ \mu\text{m}$) and POCO EDM-180 ($10\ \mu\text{m}$) electrodes and produced empirical and mathematical models relating machining parameters to MRR, relative TWR and Ra. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}  

Peak current and pulse duration emerge repeatedly as dominant drivers of MRR and surface roughness in nickel-based alloy EDM studies: Nowicki et al. identify discharge current and pulse duration as the principal factors controlling MRR and Ra for both graphite grades. {{cite:S1#c1}} Nas & Kara quantify peak current as the single most influential factor for both MRR and Ra in their Taguchi-ANOVA analysis, attributing 86.43% of MRR variance and 74.79% of Ra variance to peak current in their experiments. {{cite:S2#c1}} Akıncıoğlu similarly reports amperage as the overwhelmingly dominant factor for MRR and TWR in Titanium EDM experiments, with ANOVA percentages above 99% for several responses. {{cite:S8#c1}} Together these studies indicate that current (amperage/peak current) and pulse-on time/pulse duration are the principal process parameters affecting MRR, TWR and Ra across non-conventional-machining studies cited here. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}}  

For electrode wear specifically, Ramabalan et al. find that current and pulse duration strongly affect electrode shape loss and wear length when machining a Hastelloy variant, observing that sharp square-electrode edges are transformed to hemispherical ends under unstable sparking and heat diffusion. {{cite:S4#c1}} Shastri et al. summarise that high TWR, low MRR and coarse surface finish are common EDM challenges and list several process augmentations (cryogenic treatment, powder-mixed dielectrics, ultrasonic assistance) that researchers have used to address these issues. {{cite:S6#c1}} Sana et al. demonstrate that electrode treatments (cryogenic) and dielectric modifications can markedly reduce electrode wear and overcut in brass electrodes under their experimental conditions. {{cite:S7#c1}}  

