rows.aRaw:
Electrical discharge machining studies of nickel-based alloys emphasise the importance of electrode material properties for process efficiency, dimensional accuracy and surface finish. Nowicki et al. report that the choice of electrode material and its microstructure (specifically graphite grain size) strongly influences material removal rate (MRR), tool wear rate (TWR) and surface roughness (Ra) when machining Hastelloy C-22, and they derive empirical models relating machining parameters to these indicators. {{cite:S1#c1}}  

Two detailed experimental investigations of graphite electrodes on Hastelloy C-22 find consistent trends but add different emphases. Nowicki et al. (2024) show that finer-grained graphite increases process efficiency while reducing relative tool wear, and they attribute variations in discharge stability and process efficiency to detachment of graphite grains from the electrode. {{cite:S3#c1}} Nowicki et al. (2022) also found that larger-grain graphite delivers higher MRR but with greater relative tool wear, whereas the finest-grain electrode produced the lowest Ra; both studies identify discharge current and pulse duration as primary drivers of MRR and Ra, with the inter-pulse time most influential for relative tool wear in graphite. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}  

Work on process parameter sensitivity across other high-performance alloys reinforces the dominant role of peak current and pulse duration in controlling MRR, TWR and surface quality. Nas & Kara applied a full-factorial design and Taguchi gray relational analysis to a corrosion-resistant superalloy and found peak current to be the single most influential factor for both Ra and MRR, with optimal combinations involving cryogenic treatment, pulse-on time and current. {{cite:S2#c1}} Ramabalan et al. quantify the effect of current and pulse duration on electrode geometry changes during EDM of Hastelloy B2, observing that unstable sparks drive rapid edge rounding and wear that transform sharp square electrodes into hemispherical ends. {{cite:S4#c1}} Akıncıoğlu reports similarly high sensitivity of MRR and TWR to amperage in experiments on titanium, with amperage dominating ANOVA results for multiple performance measures. {{cite:S8#c1}}  

Researchers also explore process modifications and alternative electrode or dielectric strategies to reduce TWR and improve accuracy. Reviews and comparative studies highlight powder-mixed EDM, cryogenic treatments and hybrid assistance methods as routes to alter MRR, TWR and surface integrity, and one experimental study using nanopowder and treated brass electrodes reports substantial reductions in electrode wear and overcut under specific dielectric and treatment conditions. {{cite:S6#c1}}{{cite:S7#c1}} Liew et al. summarise electrical discharge coating approaches that modify surface functions via discharges, indicating broader interest in discharge-mediated surface engineering. {{cite:S5#c1}}  

Despite these contributions, direct comparative evidence on composite electrodes versus conventional copper or graphite electrodes specifically for Hastelloy alloys is not present in the surveyed material. [[NEEDS SOURCE: comparison of composite electrodes for Hastelloy]]

rows.bRaw:
The experiments on Hastelloy C-22 found that graphite electrodes with larger grain size increased material removal rate while also producing higher relative tool wear, and that the finest surface finish came from the smaller-grain graphite; discharge current and pulse duration were identified as the principal controls on MRR and Ra, while the time interval (off-time) dominated relative graphite wear. {{cite:S1#c1}}

A subsequent study that also machined Hastelloy C-22 with negative polarity corroborated that using fine-grained graphite raises process efficiency and lowers tool wear, and it further reported that graphite grains detaching from the electrode influence discharge stability and thereby the machining effectiveness; the authors translated their experimental results into predictive mathematical models relating electrode grain size and process settings to MRR, TWR and Ra. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Work on a different Hastelloy variant (B2) emphasised the sensitivity of electrode geometry and wear to the thermal action of the sparks: higher current and longer pulse duration markedly increased electrode wear and rounded initially sharp electrode edges into hemispherical ends, with practical consequences for producing square holes where electrode wear changes hole geometry. {{cite:S4#c1}}

A factorial study that varied pulse-on time and peak current while comparing untreated and cryogenically treated superalloy samples showed that peak current dominated both surface roughness and material removal rate by large margins, and that an experimental combination with shallow cryogenic treatment, low peak current (6 A) and short pulse-on (300 μs) optimised the joint Ra–MRR objective according to Taguchi-gray relational analysis. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Reviews of EDM methods emphasise common process weaknesses—high tool wear, low MRR and elevated surface roughness—and summarise several intervention strategies that have been tested to mitigate these issues, including cryogenic electrode treatment, powder-mixed dielectric, ultrasonic assistance and other hybrid approaches. {{cite:S6#c1}} Experimental work using nanopowder-mixed dielectrics and cryogenically treated brass electrodes reported substantial reductions in electrode wear rate and improvements in dimensional accuracy for those electrode–dielectric combinations, demonstrating that electrode treatment and dielectric modification can strongly change EWR and overcut even where the electrode material differs from the workpiece. {{cite:S7#c1}}

