Selective Laser Melting (SLM) produces near-full-density metallic parts by layerwise melting and fusion of powder using a high power-density laser, a process that enables near net-shape components and has been applied across many metals. {{cite:S1#c1}} The layerwise energy input and extreme local cooling in SLM create characteristic hierarchical microstructures with very fine subgrains and precipitates that differ from conventionally processed steels; these AM-specific features can enhance strength, wear resistance and corrosion resistance in some steels while posing challenges for ductility and fatigue. {{cite:S6#c1}} Evidence from MS300 produced by SLM shows that process choice also affects densification and porosity: SLM composites achieved higher densification and lower porosity than cold-sprayed counterparts, and such differences influence mechanical and surface-response properties. {{cite:S7#c1}}

As-built SLM maraging steel typically exhibits a very fine martensitic microstructure produced by rapid solidification and cooling. {{cite:S3#c1}} For example, SLM fabrication of grade 300 maraging steel produced submicron grain sizes consistent with cooling rates on the order of 10^7 K/s and a martensitic matrix containing needle-shaped Ni3X nanoprecipitates that contribute to age hardening. {{cite:S3#c1}} Detailed EBSD work on SLM maraging steel reports a fine martensitic structure in multiple build-section orientations and, despite the layerwise build, no strong preferred texture was observed in one study. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Post-process heat treatments alter both the precipitation state and phase content, with consequences for mechanical and electrochemical behaviour. {{cite:S3#c1}} Aging produces extensive precipitation that maximizes hardness and affects corrosion potential, whereas specific intercritical tempering can induce martensite-to-austenite reversion (reversed austenite) which concentrates Ni locally and nucleates along martensite lath boundaries. {{cite:S3#c1}} Intercritical tempering that produced reversed austenite at ~720 °C led to improved pitting performance in 3.5 wt% NaCl: the tempered specimens with reversed austenite showed higher pitting potential, lower corrosion current density and lower corrosion rate than as-printed, aged, or homogenized samples without reversed austenite. {{cite:S4#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}

Surface condition and subsequent thermochemical or electrochemical surface treatments further influence corrosion behaviour. {{cite:S2#c1}} Plasma nitriding applied to SLM 18Ni-300, when performed without prior aging, produced brittle cracks in the nitride compound layer, whereas nitriding after appropriate prior heat treatment improved wear and corrosion resistance relative to untreated SLM material. {{cite:S2#c1}} Electropolishing significantly reduces the high as-printed surface roughness (Ra ≈ 10 µm) of SLM maraging steel and was shown to lower Ra by about 65%, with precipitation state controlling both nano-hardness and corrosion response in heat-treated conditions. {{cite:S8#c1}} Overall, studies agree that SLM-induced fine microstructure and precipitation strongly interact with heat treatment and surface finishes to determine corrosion performance in chloride environments. {{cite:S6#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}

rows.bRaw:
The as-built selective laser melting (SLM) maraging steel commonly presents an ultrafine martensitic matrix with submicron grains: an average grain size of 0.31 μm was reported and attributed to cooling rates on the order of $10^{7}\ \mathrm{K/s}$. {{cite:S3#c1}} The same SLM condition produces a dense, near-full-density part when process parameters are optimised, which explains why studies treat the as-built microstructure as a fine, largely continuous martensitic network rather than a highly porous skeleton. {{cite:S1#c1}} Electron-backscatter-diffraction mapping in three orthogonal sections showed a consistent fine martensitic structure across orientations in as-printed samples, with no pronounced crystallographic texture or anisotropy in the microstructure. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Aging and solution-type heat treatments alter both precipitate state and residual stress: large populations of needle-shaped Ni3X precipitates (X = Ti, Al, Mo) form within the martensitic matrix during appropriate ageing, and these precipitates are responsible for age hardening through mechanisms described as Orowan bowing and coherency strain. {{cite:S3#c1}} Heat treatments also substantially relieve residual stresses induced by the layer-wise build and reduce, but do not entirely eliminate, the mechanical anisotropy associated with build orientation. {{cite:S3#c1}} In comparative studies, heat-treated SLM maraging steel showed hardness and corrosion responses similar to conventionally processed equivalents when equivalent thermal cycles were applied, indicating that precipitation state largely controls these properties post-heat-treatment. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Intercritical tempering near the austenite-stability window produces reversed austenite that is spatially localised at martensite lath boundaries: tempering at about $720\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$ generated reversed austenite precipitates primarily along lath boundaries, nucleated where local Ni enrichment followed dissolution of intermetallics. {{cite:S4#c1}} That specific microstructural change correlated with electrochemical improvements measured in a chloride-containing electrolyte: the $720\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$-tempered specimens exhibited a higher pitting potential and a lower corrosion current density compared with as-printed, conventionally aged, and homogenised samples lacking reversed austenite. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Thermo-chemical surface modification interacts with prior heat treatments: plasma nitriding carried out at $520\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$ for 6 hours produced damaged nitride compound layers when applied without prior ageing, whereas performing nitriding after solution and aging treatments mitigated cracking and improved wear and corrosion resistance of the additive-manufactured material. {{cite:S2#c1}} This indicates that the bulk precipitation and phase state prior to nitriding govern the integrity and protective function of the nitride layer. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Surface condition and roughness remain key factors for corrosion behaviour in chloride environments: as-printed samples display a high surface roughness (Ra ≈ 10 μm), and electropolishing reduced Ra by about 65%, while ageing controlled precipitation and thereby influenced both hardness and corrosion potential. {{cite:S8#c1}} Taken together, the literature links three scales of microstructural control to chloride-induced corrosion performance in SLM maraging steel: nanoscale precipitate chemistry and distribution, mesoscale reversed-austenite formation at lath boundaries, and the macroscopic surface state shaped by as-built roughness and post-processing. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}}

rows.aRaw:
Selective Laser Melting (SLM) creates near-full-density metal parts by layerwise melting and fusion of powder, producing components with up to 99.9% relative density when processing parameters are optimised. {{cite:S1#c1}} This manufacturing route imposes very high cooling rates that produce a distinctive, very fine martensitic microstructure in grade 300 maraging steel: one study reports submicron grains with an average size of $0.31\ \mu$m and infers cooling on the order of $10^{7}\ \mathrm{K/s}$. {{cite:S3#c1}} Electron microscopy in the same work identified abundant needle-shaped Ni$_3$X (X = Ti, Al, Mo) nanoprecipitates in the martensitic matrix that are directly implicated in age hardening. {{cite:S3#c1}} Reviews of AM steels emphasise that these hierarchical subgrain structures and fine precipitates arise from the layerwise thermal cycles and act as an intrinsic heat treatment that can increase strength, wear resistance and corrosion resistance compared with conventionally produced counterparts. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Post-build heat treatments alter both mechanical and corrosion-related properties by changing precipitate state, residual stress and phase constitution. Systematic characterisation of as-built and heat-treated SLM maraging steel shows that ageing maximises hardness and relieves residual stress, while solution treatments and intercritical tempering can induce phase changes such as austenite reversion. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}} Aging is also identified as the principal microstructural driver of both hardness and corrosion response: precipitation dominates the observed shifts in nano-hardness and in corrosion potential after heat treatment. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Austenite reversion produced by intercritical tempering near the $\alpha'+\gamma$ range modifies corrosion behaviour in chloride-containing solutions: specimens tempered at $720\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$ developed reversed austenite concentrated along martensite lath boundaries, attributed to local Ni enrichment from intermetallic dissolution, and these specimens showed higher pitting potential and lower corrosion current density in $3.5\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ NaCl than as-printed, aged, or homogenised samples. {{cite:S4#c1}} At the same time, thermochemical treatments interact with prior heat history: plasma nitriding applied without prior ageing produced cracking in the compound nitride layer, whereas nitriding following prior heat treatments improved wear and corrosion resistance of SLM 18Ni-300 compared with untreated AM material. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Surface state and roughness from SLM also affect corrosion outcomes. As-printed maraging steel presents a fine martensitic structure with an Ra of about $10\ \mu$m, and electropolishing that reduces Ra by roughly 65% was shown to influence measured corrosion behaviour; after heat treatment the corrosion behaviour of SLM and wrought maraging steel converged in the reported comparisons. {{cite:S8#c1}} Broad reviews note that the many process parameters in SLM (building orientation, heat treatment, energy density, surface quality) complicate generalisation of fatigue and corrosion performance and leave gaps in comprehensive understanding. {{cite:S5#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}}

[[NEEDS SOURCE: quantitative comparisons of pitting rates across heat treatments]]

rows.bRaw:
SLM can produce near–full-density maraging-steel parts, with reported relative densities up to 99.9%. {{cite:S1#c1}} This near-full densification combines with the layerwise, high-energy melting and rapid solidification of SLM to produce extremely fine microstructures and in situ thermal cycles that generate hierarchical subgrains and precipitation phenomena not typically seen in conventionally made steels. {{cite:S6#c1}} In one detailed study of SLM 300-grade maraging steel, an average submicron grain size of $0.31\ \mu\text{m}$ was measured and cooling rates were estimated up to $10^{7}\ \text{K/s}$; needle-shaped Ni$_3$X precipitates (X = Ti, Al, Mo) were observed within a martensitic matrix and linked to age hardening. {{cite:S3#c1}}

As-built SLM maraging steel has been described as a fine-grained martensitic structure with substantial surface roughness and little crystallographic texture across build sections. {{cite:S8#c1}} The same work quantified the as-printed surface roughness as about $Ra=10\ \mu\text{m}$ and reported that heat treatment changes both hardness and corrosion potential, with precipitation identified as the dominant influence on those properties. {{cite:S8#c1}} Heat treatments applied to SLM parts relieve residual stresses and modify mechanical anisotropy introduced by the layerwise build, while also altering precipitation and strengthening mechanisms observed in the as-fabricated condition. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Intercritical heat treatment that produces reversed austenite has a measurable effect on chloride-induced pitting: tempering within the mixed martensite–austenite range at $720^\circ\text{C}$ caused reversed austenite to form mainly along martensite-lath boundaries, nucleated where Ni is locally enriched after dissolution of intermetallics, and this microstructural change increased pitting potential and reduced corrosion current density in 3.5 wt% NaCl. {{cite:S4#c1}} While that study demonstrates improved pitting resistance when reversed austenite is present, the broader influence of martensite-to-austenite reversion on corrosion across different SLM conditions remains incompletely resolved. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Thermo-chemical surface treatments and aging interact with prior heat treatments to affect corrosion behaviour: plasma nitriding applied without prior aging produced cracks in the nitride compound layer, whereas nitriding after appropriate solution and aging treatments improved wear and corrosion resistance relative to untreated SLM material and to conventionally produced counterparts subjected to the same treatments. {{cite:S2#c1}} Aging in SLM maraging steel was reported to give the highest nano-hardness and the lowest corrosion potential among the tested conditions, and electropolishing can reduce the as-printed surface roughness by about 65%, which is relevant because surface finish influences corrosion performance. {{cite:S8#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}}

