The pragmatic randomized trial found no difference in HbA1c between intervention and control arms at 3 months (mean difference $-0.42$, 95% CI $-1.05$ to $0.21$, $P=.19$). {{cite:S6#c1}} The same trial reported low overall app usage but observed an association within users: each additional day of app use corresponded with a $0.016$-point decrease in 3-month HbA1c (95% CI $-0.03$ to $-0.003$), indicating a dose–response between engagement and glycaemic change in this sample. {{cite:S6#c1}}

A synthesis of 23 studies found that 17 reported statistically significant effects on targeted health behaviors, with intervention retention in the studies generally high (65% or greater in 19 studies, range 60%–100%). {{cite:S4#c1}} That review also identifies common features linked to greater effectiveness: low time burden, intuitive interfaces, real-time feedback, tailoring to the individual, detailed content, and involvement of health professionals. {{cite:S4#c1}}

A qualitative study of 44 smartphone owners identified nine user-expressed themes that shaped adoption and continued use, including barriers and motivators, the need for personalized information and credible content, tracking for awareness, goal setting, reminders, and concerns about sharing personal information. {{cite:S5#c1}} These user-derived priorities map onto the behavior-change techniques observed in trials (for example, self-monitoring) and suggest design levers that may sustain engagement. {{cite:S5#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}

A pilot evaluation in adolescents with diabetes showed that an app incorporating automated glucometer transfer, social features and gamified rewards increased average daily blood glucose checks by 50% (from 2.4 to 3.6 per day; $P=.006$, $n=12$) and high satisfaction (88% willing to continue use), while noting that demonstration of HbA1c improvement would require larger, longer trials. {{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}} This finding illustrates that specific features—automation, immediate feedback and incentive mechanisms—can raise short-term self-monitoring frequency even when clinical outcomes remain unproven in small pilots. {{cite:S2#c1}}

An audit of commercial diabetes apps found that only a minority scored highly across eight evaluated self-management features: of the apps meeting inclusion criteria, 11 (31.4%) achieved a score of 5 while 7 (20%) scored 3, and the sampled apps tended to prioritise recording and reminders over personalised education or therapeutic support. {{cite:S7#c1}} This gap between common app capabilities and user-desired functions identified in qualitative work helps explain why many offerings fail to sustain use. {{cite:S7#c1}}{{cite:S5#c1}}

An appraisal of the app ecosystem estimated tens of thousands of health apps and highlights widespread concerns about variable quality and the need for mechanisms to assess content, usability, connectivity and privacy. {{cite:S3#c1}} Complementing that landscape-level concern, interviews with people with type 2 diabetes show that most app users report improvements in self-management, that health professional recommendation increases satisfaction, and that non-users commonly never had apps suggested by clinicians. {{cite:S8#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}} Together these findings underscore that both app design and clinical endorsement shape engagement and therefore the likelihood of observed clinical benefit. {{cite:S8#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}

