The quantitative comparison finds that women running microfinance-backed businesses show higher scores on economic, psychological and some political empowerment indicators than non-entrepreneur women in the same sample, though political and social gains are smaller. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Building on that, the qualitative interviews in the same study confirm that entrepreneurship supported by microfinance correlates with broader empowerment, but they also show that many non-entrepreneurs already display some social empowerment, and that control of the enterprise by the woman herself is important for realizing empowerment benefits. {{cite:S1#c1}}

A systematic review of empirical work finds mixed results on whether access to microfinance increases women's empowerment, and proposes that empowerment unfolds on three dimensions: personal beliefs and actions (micro), relations with others (meso), and broader societal outcomes (macro). {{cite:S6#c1}} The review further argues that timing and culture shape which dimensions change and when, and that programmes should specify which dimension(s) they aim to affect. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Evidence from a seven-year quasi-experiment in rural India shows that social ties and information technologies interact with interventions to affect entrepreneurial take-up and profits: stronger ties to family and community raise ICT use and entrepreneurship, while ties to men in local authority can suppress ICT benefits; the ICT intervention generated 160 new businesses in treated villages versus 40 in controls. {{cite:S7#c1}} These results imply that finance alone may be insufficient where social networks and information access shape women's ability to start and scale enterprises. {{cite:S7#c1}}

Ethnographic work in Bangladesh reports that microfinance can increase indebtedness among already poor households and may exacerbate economic, social and environmental vulnerabilities, indicating potential negative consequences when credit is promoted without complementary supports. {{cite:S5#c1}} This finding contrasts with studies highlighting empowerment gains and suggests heterogeneous effects across contexts and implementation. {{cite:S5#c1}}

Reviews of the microfinance sector stress that institutional form and funding matter for whom services reach: commercially oriented providers expand but may not serve the poorest, while mission-driven institutions—sometimes subsidized—are more likely to reach very poor clients at scale. {{cite:S2#c1}} A finance literature review similarly notes that microfinance sits apart from mainstream entrepreneurial finance and has diverse products, practices and impact assessments that influence outcomes. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Studies of socially embedded entrepreneurship in India and of women owner-managers in tourism indicate that business processes aligned with local norms can both enable economic activity and shift social relations, supporting the idea that interventions combining economic opportunity with sensitivity to social norms can change women's positions. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}}

