Khan et al. 2022 report that women running microfinance-backed businesses show higher scores on economic, psychological and some empowerment indicators compared with non-entrepreneur women in their sample, while gains in political and social dimensions are smaller. {{cite:S1#c1}} This quantitative result is corroborated by interviews in the same study, which indicate that entrepreneurship can contribute to "holistic empowerment" when women exercise full control over their ventures, but the study cautions about limited external validity given its geographic scope and data-collection constraints. {{cite:S1#c1}} 

Banerjee & Jackson 2016 find that microfinance can increase indebtedness and exacerbate economic and social vulnerabilities in poor communities, undermining the conditions in which entrepreneurship might generate sustained household welfare gains. {{cite:S5#c1}} This ethnographic evidence suggests that providing credit without addressing repayment pressures and community-level consequences can limit the net benefits of microfinance for households. {{cite:S5#c1}}

Cull et al. 2009 show that the structure of the microfinance industry matters for whom services reach: institutions with explicit social missions, often supported by subsidies, are the ones more able to serve the poorest customers, whereas purely profit-driven providers are unlikely to target the same clientele. {{cite:S2#c1}} Brau & Woller 2004 similarly emphasize that microfinance is distinct from mainstream entrepreneurial finance and that questions of MFI sustainability, product design, and targeting affect impact assessments. {{cite:S4#c1}} Together these industry-level findings imply that institutional incentives and product design shape whether credit translates into business growth and household decision-making changes. {{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}

Huis et al. 2017 synthesize the mixed empirical evidence by proposing a Three-Dimensional Model of Women's Empowerment—personal, relational and societal—and argue that time and culture influence when and where different dimensions respond to interventions. {{cite:S6#c1}} This model helps explain why studies observe improvements in individual beliefs or actions but weaker shifts in household power relations or broader societal norms: effects can appear first at the personal level and only later, or not at all, at relational or societal levels depending on context and intervention design. {{cite:S6#c1}} 

Venkatesh et al. 2017 document that augmenting economic inputs with information and communication technology can materially increase business starts and profits in rural Indian villages, and that social ties modulate these effects: family and community ties amplify benefits, while ties to male power-holders can impede them unless ICT use is high. {{cite:S7#c1}} Haugh & Talwar 2014 find that business processes which align with local norms can enable women's economic activity and contribute to shifting social orders, indicating that non-financial adaptations matter for both enterprise outcomes and empowerment. {{cite:S7#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}} 

